Maybe this is a spoiler, but most of it is pretty obvious:


First you need a Wand of Polymorph, or a spell of Polymorph.

Then you need a big pile of stuff.  And preferably an altar, so you
can identify its curse status if you're not a priest.  You don't want
the pile to have too many items in it, though, or you'll create golems
out of some of the stuff.

Make sure nothing too valuable is in the pile, and then zap it.  You'll
find a few things:

1. The number of items of each type (armor, tools, ...) is now <= what
   it was before.

2. Not all items change.  Sometimes you'll see that a previously ID'd
   item is still there.

3. You'll never get certain items, like a Wand of Wishing or artifacts.

Polypiling is a great way to get high-quality armor, since you find
tons of armor in areas like Fort Ludios.  It's also a nice to
get magic markers from all the useless tools you find.  It'll let you
make tons of gems out of rocks if you're patient enough, which will 
let you pad your score to infinity if you're concerned about such
things.  And you can change your 600 wands of digging into something
else.  Spellbooks are a good thing to polypile since you really only
use a couple of your spells anyway, and you might eventually get good 
ones like identify and magic mapping out of the useless once.

OTOH, you can write all the scrolls you want by erasing scrolls and
using a marker, so after a while polypiling scrolls is a bad idea.
Polypiling potions is useful to a point, but after a while you have
all the stats and levels you need and you might as well just make
holy water.  And random weapons are useless once you get your first 
artifact weapon. 

It's a powerful tactic, but not a totally cheesy one IMO unless you 
wish for a PYEC to keep recharging that WoP.

